{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019wGTS4HmuENTbktYx3PN8p","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd004-ts\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/ts: Timestamp Stdin Lines\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  Shell pipelines that process log output often need each line annotated with the time\n6\t  it arrived. The standard Unix tools offer no built-in way to prepend a timestamp to\n7\t  each stdin line. ts (from moreutils) fills this gap: it reads stdin line by line and\n8\t  writes each line to stdout prefixed with a strftime-formatted timestamp. The Go\n9\t  implementation must match the Homebrew reference binary (ts, installed by moreutils)\n10\t  byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n11\t  pkg/testutils.\n12\t\n13\tgoals:\n14\t  - G1: Prepend a strftime timestamp to each stdin line with the default format \"%b %d %H:%M:%S\".\n15\t  - G2: Support custom strftime format strings including the ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S, %.s, and %.T.\n16\t  - G3: Support incremental and elapsed-time modes (-i and -s) that show time between lines or time since start.\n17\t  - G4: Support monotonic clock mode (-m) for timestamp sources that must not jump on wall-clock adjustments.\n18\t  - G5: Support relative-time conversion mode (-r) that rewrites existing timestamps in stdin to relative age strings.\n19\t\n20\trequirements:\n21\t  R1:\n22\t    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n23\t    items:\n24\t      - R1.1:\n25\t          text: Must read stdin line by line (newline-delimited). Each line must be written to stdout prefixed by a timestamp and a single space.\n26\t      - R1.2:\n27\t          text: The default timestamp format must be \"%b %d %H:%M:%S\" (strftime, e.g. \"Feb 19 14:05:32\"). The format must be evaluated at the time each line is received, not at startup.\n28\t      - R1.3:\n29\t          text: Must flush stdout after each line so that downstream consumers receive timestamps in real time.\n30\t      - R1.4:\n31\t          text: Must preserve the original newline at the end of each line; must not add an extra newline.\n32\t      - R1.5:\n33\t          text: Must pass through partial lines (lines not terminated by a newline at EOF) with a timestamp prefix.\n34\t      - R1.6:\n35\t          text: Exit code must be 0 after stdin is closed with EOF. Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n36\t\n37\t  R2:\n38\t    title: Custom format string\n39\t    items:\n40\t      - R2.1:\n41\t          text: Must accept an optional positional argument specifying a custom strftime format string, e.g. \"ts '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'\".\n42\t      - R2.2:\n43\t          text: Must support all standard strftime(3) conversion specifications available on the target platform.\n44\t      - R2.3:\n45\t          text: 'Must support three ts-specific subsecond extensions: %.S (seconds with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"32.001234\"), %.s (Unix epoch with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"1708358732.001234\"), %.T (HH:MM:SS with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"14:05:32.001234\"). These extensions require Time::HiRes in the Perl reference; the Go implementation must use time.Now() sub-nanosecond precision formatted to six decimal places.'\n46\t      - R2.4:\n47\t          text: When a subsecond extension is used in the format string, must obtain both the second and microsecond components atomically from a single time sample per line.\n48\t\n49\t  R3:\n50\t    title: Incremental mode (-i)\n51\t    items:\n52\t      - R3.1:\n53\t          text: -i must change the timestamp to the time elapsed since the previous line was received. The first line's timestamp is the time elapsed since ts started.\n54\t      - R3.2:\n55\t          text: The default format in -i mode must be \"%H:%M:%S\". The TZ environment must be set to GMT so that strftime on a delta-second value produces a correct elapsed string (e.g., 5 seconds since last line → \"00:00:05\").\n56\t      - R3.3:\n57\t          text: A custom format argument overrides the -i default format. The TZ=GMT behavior still applies.\n58\t      - R3.4:\n59\t          text: -i and -s are mutually exclusive; passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero.\n60\t\n61\t  R4:\n62\t    title: Elapsed-since-start mode (-s)\n63\t    items:\n64\t      - R4.1:\n65\t          text: -s must change the timestamp to the time elapsed since ts started, increasing monotonically across lines (unlike -i which resets per line).\n66\t      - R4.2:\n67\t          text: The default format in -s mode must be \"%H:%M:%S\" with TZ=GMT, same as -i.\n68\t      - R4.3:\n69\t          text: A custom format argument overrides the -s default format.\n70\t\n71\t  R5:\n72\t    title: Monotonic clock mode (-m)\n73\t    items:\n74\t      - R5.1:\n75\t          text: -m must use the system's monotonic clock (CLOCK_MONOTONIC via time.Now() in Go, which automatically uses the monotonic reading) instead of the wall clock for timestamp sampling.\n76\t      - R5.2:\n77\t          text: -m is compatible with all format modes (-i, -s, default) and with custom format strings including subsecond extensions.\n78\t      - R5.3:\n79\t          text: When -m is active, the timestamp must not jump backwards or exhibit discontinuities if the wall clock is adjusted (e.g., by NTP) while ts is running.\n80\t\n81\t  R6:\n82\t    title: Relative-time parsing and age output\n83\t    items:\n84\t      - R6.1:\n85\t          text: -r must scan each input line for known timestamp patterns and replace each match with a human-readable relative age string (e.g., \"15m5s ago\").\n86\t      - R6.2:\n87\t          text: 'Must recognize the following timestamp patterns from the Perl source regex: syslog format (\"Jan  5 14:30:00\"), ISO-8601 (\"2024-01-05T14:30:00.000Z\"), RFC 2822 with optional day (\"16 Jun 94 07:29:35 GMT\"), lastlog format (\"Mon Jan  5 14:30\").'\n88\t\n89\t  R10:\n90\t    title: Relative-time format override and integration\n91\t    items:\n92\t      - R10.1:\n93\t          text: When -r and a format string are both given, must convert matched timestamps to the specified format via strftime rather than to a relative string.\n94\t      - R10.2:\n95\t          text: Lines with no recognizable timestamp must be passed through unchanged.\n96\t      - R10.3:\n97\t          text: -r is mutually exclusive with -i and -s; passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero.\n98\t\n99\t  R7:\n100\t    title: Exit codes and error handling\n101\t    items:\n102\t      - R7.1:\n103\t          text: Must exit 0 on clean EOF from stdin.\n104\t      - R7.2:\n105\t          text: Must exit non-zero and print a usage message to stderr when an unrecognized flag is given.\n106\t      - R7.3:\n107\t          text: Must exit non-zero when -r mode is requested but the timestamp parsing dependency (equivalent of Date::Parse) is unavailable. In the Go implementation this means the parsing library must be compiled in, so this condition cannot arise; document the invariant in the implementation.\n108\t\n109\t  R8:\n110\t    title: Environment\n111\t    items:\n112\t      - R8.1:\n113\t          text: Must respect the TZ environment variable to determine the local time zone for wall-clock timestamps (non -i/-s modes). Setting TZ=UTC must cause timestamps to be in UTC.\n114\t      - R8.2:\n115\t          text: In -i and -s modes, must use TZ=GMT internally for elapsed-time formatting regardless of the user's TZ setting, matching the Perl source behavior.\n116\t\n117\t  R9:\n118\t    title: Differential testing\n119\t    items:\n120\t      - R9.1:\n121\t          text: |\n122\t            Differential tests must use testutils.TimestampNormalizer (type func([]byte) []byte, defined in pkg/testutils) to replace wall-clock timestamps in output with a fixed placeholder before comparing Go and reference binary outputs. Pass it in the DiffTest.Normalize slice. The normalizer matches strftime-formatted patterns such as \"Feb 19 14:05:32\" and replaces them with a fixed string so that wall-clock differences do not cause test failures.\n123\t      - R9.2:\n124\t          text: 'Tests must cover: default format, custom strftime format, -s mode, -i mode, -m mode (with monotonic normalization), multi-line stdin, empty stdin, and partial last line (no trailing newline).'\n125\t\n126\tnon_goals:\n127\t  - cmd/ts does not implement egrep-style timestamp colorization.\n128\t  - cmd/ts does not read from files; it reads only from stdin.\n129\t  - cmd/ts does not support localized date parsing in -r mode (the Perl source explicitly notes this limitation).\n130\t  - cmd/ts does not implement --help or --version flags beyond a usage string on bad input.\n131\t\n132\tacceptance_criteria:\n133\t  - id: AC1\n134\t    criterion: \"echo 'hello world' | go run ./cmd/ts produces a line matching the pattern: '<month> <day> <HH:MM:SS> hello world'.\"\n135\t    traces:\n136\t      - R1.1\n137\t      - R1.2\n138\t      - R1.3\n139\t      - R1.4\n140\t      - R1.5\n141\t      - R1.6\n142\t  - id: AC2\n143\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/ts -s produces three lines with monotonically increasing elapsed timestamps.\"\n144\t    traces:\n145\t      - R3.4\n146\t      - R4.1\n147\t      - R4.2\n148\t      - R4.3\n149\t      - R5.2\n150\t      - R8.1\n151\t      - R8.2\n152\t      - R9.2\n153\t  - id: AC3\n154\t    criterion: \"Differential test against ts reference binary passes with TimestampNormalizer applied for default, -s, and -i modes on a 10-line stdin input.\"\n155\t    traces:\n156\t      - R1.1\n157\t      - R1.6\n158\t      - R3.1\n159\t      - R3.2\n160\t      - R3.3\n161\t      - R3.4\n162\t      - R4.1\n163\t      - R4.2\n164\t      - R4.3\n165\t      - R5.2\n166\t      - R10.3\n167\t      - R7.1\n168\t      - R8.1\n169\t      - R8.2\n170\t      - R9.1\n171\t      - R9.2\n172\t  - id: AC4\n173\t    criterion: \"ts with an invalid flag prints a usage message to stderr and exits non-zero.\"\n174\t    traces:\n175\t      - R3.4\n176\t      - R10.3\n177\t      - R7.2\n178\t  - id: AC5\n179\t    criterion: \"Empty stdin (immediate EOF) produces no output and exits 0.\"\n180\t    traces:\n181\t      - R1.1\n182\t      - R1.6\n183\t      - R3.4\n184\t      - R10.3\n185\t      - R7.1\n186\t      - R7.2\n187\t      - R7.3\n188\t      - R9.2\n189\t  - id: AC6\n190\t    criterion: \"cmd/ts compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n191\t    traces:\n192\t      - R1.1\n193\t      - R1.2\n194\t      - R1.3\n195\t      - R1.4\n196\t      - R1.5\n197\t      - R1.6\n198\t      - R2.1\n199\t      - R2.2\n200\t      - R2.3\n201\t      - R2.4\n202\t      - R3.1\n203\t      - R3.2\n204\t      - R3.3\n205\t      - R3.4\n206\t      - R4.1\n207\t      - R4.2\n208\t      - R4.3\n209\t      - R5.1\n210\t      - R5.2\n211\t      - R5.3\n212\t      - R6.1\n213\t      - R6.2\n214\t      - R7.1\n215\t      - R7.2\n216\t      - R7.3\n217\t      - R8.1\n218\t      - R8.2\n219\t      - R9.1\n220\t      - R9.2\n221\t      - R10.1\n222\t      - R10.2\n223\t      - R10.3\n224\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"efaf62f2-c8ce-4b4c-8948-b45a93d595ca","uuid":"7d8e6395-80b6-45a4-b736-5bbc6eeff53d","timestamp":"2026-05-29T16:46:24.462Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5652/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd004-ts.yaml","content":"id: srd004-ts\ntitle: \"cmd/ts: Timestamp Stdin Lines\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Shell pipelines that process log output often need each line annotated with the time\n  it arrived. The standard Unix tools offer no built-in way to prepend a timestamp to\n  each stdin line. ts (from moreutils) fills this gap: it reads stdin line by line and\n  writes each line to stdout prefixed with a strftime-formatted timestamp. The Go\n  implementation must match the Homebrew reference binary (ts, installed by moreutils)\n  byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential testing via\n  pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Prepend a strftime timestamp to each stdin line with the default format \"%b %d %H:%M:%S\".\n  - G2: Support custom strftime format strings including the ts-specific subsecond extensions %.S, %.s, and %.T.\n  - G3: Support incremental and elapsed-time modes (-i and -s) that show time between lines or time since start.\n  - G4: Support monotonic clock mode (-m) for timestamp sources that must not jump on wall-clock adjustments.\n  - G5: Support relative-time conversion mode (-r) that rewrites existing timestamps in stdin to relative age strings.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default behavior (no flags)\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must read stdin line by line (newline-delimited). Each line must be written to stdout prefixed by a timestamp and a single space.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: The default timestamp format must be \"%b %d %H:%M:%S\" (strftime, e.g. \"Feb 19 14:05:32\"). The format must be evaluated at the time each line is received, not at startup.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Must flush stdout after each line so that downstream consumers receive timestamps in real time.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: Must preserve the original newline at the end of each line; must not add an extra newline.\n      - R1.5:\n          text: Must pass through partial lines (lines not terminated by a newline at EOF) with a timestamp prefix.\n      - R1.6:\n          text: Exit code must be 0 after stdin is closed with EOF. Must not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Custom format string\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: Must accept an optional positional argument specifying a custom strftime format string, e.g. \"ts '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S'\".\n      - R2.2:\n          text: Must support all standard strftime(3) conversion specifications available on the target platform.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: 'Must support three ts-specific subsecond extensions: %.S (seconds with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"32.001234\"), %.s (Unix epoch with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"1708358732.001234\"), %.T (HH:MM:SS with microsecond suffix, e.g. \"14:05:32.001234\"). These extensions require Time::HiRes in the Perl reference; the Go implementation must use time.Now() sub-nanosecond precision formatted to six decimal places.'\n      - R2.4:\n          text: When a subsecond extension is used in the format string, must obtain both the second and microsecond components atomically from a single time sample per line.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Incremental mode (-i)\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: -i must change the timestamp to the time elapsed since the previous line was received. The first line's timestamp is the time elapsed since ts started.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: The default format in -i mode must be \"%H:%M:%S\". The TZ environment must be set to GMT so that strftime on a delta-second value produces a correct elapsed string (e.g., 5 seconds since last line → \"00:00:05\").\n      - R3.3:\n          text: A custom format argument overrides the -i default format. The TZ=GMT behavior still applies.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: -i and -s are mutually exclusive; passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Elapsed-since-start mode (-s)\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: -s must change the timestamp to the time elapsed since ts started, increasing monotonically across lines (unlike -i which resets per line).\n      - R4.2:\n          text: The default format in -s mode must be \"%H:%M:%S\" with TZ=GMT, same as -i.\n      - R4.3:\n          text: A custom format argument overrides the -s default format.\n\n  R5:\n    title: Monotonic clock mode (-m)\n    items:\n      - R5.1:\n          text: -m must use the system's monotonic clock (CLOCK_MONOTONIC via time.Now() in Go, which automatically uses the monotonic reading) instead of the wall clock for timestamp sampling.\n      - R5.2:\n          text: -m is compatible with all format modes (-i, -s, default) and with custom format strings including subsecond extensions.\n      - R5.3:\n          text: When -m is active, the timestamp must not jump backwards or exhibit discontinuities if the wall clock is adjusted (e.g., by NTP) while ts is running.\n\n  R6:\n    title: Relative-time parsing and age output\n    items:\n      - R6.1:\n          text: -r must scan each input line for known timestamp patterns and replace each match with a human-readable relative age string (e.g., \"15m5s ago\").\n      - R6.2:\n          text: 'Must recognize the following timestamp patterns from the Perl source regex: syslog format (\"Jan  5 14:30:00\"), ISO-8601 (\"2024-01-05T14:30:00.000Z\"), RFC 2822 with optional day (\"16 Jun 94 07:29:35 GMT\"), lastlog format (\"Mon Jan  5 14:30\").'\n\n  R10:\n    title: Relative-time format override and integration\n    items:\n      - R10.1:\n          text: When -r and a format string are both given, must convert matched timestamps to the specified format via strftime rather than to a relative string.\n      - R10.2:\n          text: Lines with no recognizable timestamp must be passed through unchanged.\n      - R10.3:\n          text: -r is mutually exclusive with -i and -s; passing both must print a usage error to stderr and exit non-zero.\n\n  R7:\n    title: Exit codes and error handling\n    items:\n      - R7.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 on clean EOF from stdin.\n      - R7.2:\n          text: Must exit non-zero and print a usage message to stderr when an unrecognized flag is given.\n      - R7.3:\n          text: Must exit non-zero when -r mode is requested but the timestamp parsing dependency (equivalent of Date::Parse) is unavailable. In the Go implementation this means the parsing library must be compiled in, so this condition cannot arise; document the invariant in the implementation.\n\n  R8:\n    title: Environment\n    items:\n      - R8.1:\n          text: Must respect the TZ environment variable to determine the local time zone for wall-clock timestamps (non -i/-s modes). Setting TZ=UTC must cause timestamps to be in UTC.\n      - R8.2:\n          text: In -i and -s modes, must use TZ=GMT internally for elapsed-time formatting regardless of the user's TZ setting, matching the Perl source behavior.\n\n  R9:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R9.1:\n          text: |\n            Differential tests must use testutils.TimestampNormalizer (type func([]byte) []byte, defined in pkg/testutils) to replace wall-clock timestamps in output with a fixed placeholder before comparing Go and reference binary outputs. Pass it in the DiffTest.Normalize slice. The normalizer matches strftime-formatted patterns such as \"Feb 19 14:05:32\" and replaces them with a fixed string so that wall-clock differences do not cause test failures.\n      - R9.2:\n          text: 'Tests must cover: default format, custom strftime format, -s mode, -i mode, -m mode (with monotonic normalization), multi-line stdin, empty stdin, and partial last line (no trailing newline).'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/ts does not implement egrep-style timestamp colorization.\n  - cmd/ts does not read from files; it reads only from stdin.\n  - cmd/ts does not support localized date parsing in -r mode (the Perl source explicitly notes this limitation).\n  - cmd/ts does not implement --help or --version flags beyond a usage string on bad input.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"echo 'hello world' | go run ./cmd/ts produces a line matching the pattern: '<month> <day> <HH:MM:SS> hello world'.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n      - R1.6\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/ts -s produces three lines with monotonically increasing elapsed timestamps.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R5.2\n      - R8.1\n      - R8.2\n      - R9.2\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"Differential test against ts reference binary passes with TimestampNormalizer applied for default, -s, and -i modes on a 10-line stdin input.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.6\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R5.2\n      - R10.3\n      - R7.1\n      - R8.1\n      - R8.2\n      - R9.1\n      - R9.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"ts with an invalid flag prints a usage message to stderr and exits non-zero.\"\n    traces:\n      - R3.4\n      - R10.3\n      - R7.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Empty stdin (immediate EOF) produces no output and exits 0.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.6\n      - R3.4\n      - R10.3\n      - R7.1\n      - R7.2\n      - R7.3\n      - R9.2\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/ts compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R1.5\n      - R1.6\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n      - R5.1\n      - R5.2\n      - R5.3\n      - R6.1\n      - R6.2\n      - R7.1\n      - R7.2\n      - R7.3\n      - R8.1\n      - R8.2\n      - R9.1\n      - R9.2\n      - R10.1\n      - R10.2\n      - R10.3\n","numLines":224,"startLine":1,"totalLines":224}}}
